60,3,'Respect the achievement but honestly the math notation slows me down a lot. Dipping in and out of specific sections works better for me than reading straight through.','2026-09-17 03:00:00'),(255,24,161,5,'This is the book I wish someone handed me on my first job. The dependency-breaking techniques actually work on the messy codebase I deal with at work.','2026-09-16 15:45:00'),(256,9,161,4,'Still working through this one but already changed how I approach a legacy module I\'ve been avoiding for months. Practical in a way a lot of engineering books aren\'t.','2026-09-18 02:15:00'),(257,7,162,4,'Finally understand why some of my code runs so much slower than I expected. The chapters on the memory hierarchy alone were worth the read.','2026-09-17 06:30:00'),(258,2,163,5,'Eloquent JavaScript is exactly what I needed coming from a non-CS background. The writing style makes it feel more like a conversation than a textbook.','2026-09-16 13:50:00'),(259,26,163,4,'Working through the exercises slowly but the pixel art editor project in the later chapters is a great way to actually apply what you\'re learning.','2026-09-19 10:10:00'),(260,12,164,5,'This book made concepts like eventual consistency finally click for me after years of half-understanding them from blog posts. Genuinely one of the best technical books I\'ve read.','2026-09-16 16:40:00'),(261,25,164,4,'Dense but rewarding. The chapters on replication and partitioning are the clearest explanation I\'ve come across for how real distributed databases actually behave.','2026-09-20 03:30:00'),(262,4,165,5,'Petzold builds everything up from switches and wires and by the end you genuinely understand how a processor works. Rare to find a book this clear about something this complex.','2026-09-16 12:35:00'),(263,22,165,3,'Interesting approach but it took a while to get to anything I\'d call genuinely new information. Good for beginners, less so if you already know the basics of digital logic.','2026-09-21 06:00:00')